    "description": "Iran’s northern ports connect its transport system with the Caspian Sea, an inland body of water shared with neighboring states. Anzali, Nowshahr and Amirabad are established port names, while changing water levels make environmental conditions an essential part of understanding the region’s infrastructure.",
    "tags": ["Caspian Sea", "Northern ports", "Anzali", "Nowshahr", "Amirabad", "Coastal infrastructure"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:11:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:11:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Northern Ports: Caspian Gateways and Environmental Constraints | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Anzali, Nowshahr and Amirabad, their Caspian setting, and the environmental constraints affecting port infrastructure."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "caspian-setting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Ports on an inland sea",
        "content": "Iran’s northern maritime gateways face the Caspian Sea, the inland water body bordered by Iran, Azerbaijan, Kazakhstan, Russia and Turkmenistan. This setting gives them a different regional context from Iran’s southern ports. An account of northern shipping must consider conditions across the Caspian rather than assuming the operating geography of an open-ocean port. [s2]\n\nThe International Association of Ports and Harbors lists Anzali, Noshahr and Amirabad among the ports associated with Iran’s Ports and Maritime Organization, or PMO. Noshahr is another English rendering of Nowshahr. The directory also describes PMO responsibilities concerning ports, coastal facilities and maritime commerce. It establishes the ports’ institutional context, but provides neither a complete inventory of every northern facility nor a comparable account of their present cargo activities. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A network that requires more than port capacity",
        "content": "A port’s inclusion in a regional network should be distinguished from evidence of a particular scheduled service. Kazakhstan’s Aktau port maintains a partner directory that includes Amirabad and Anzali. This provides direct institutional evidence of regional port relationships, but does not establish sailing frequency, cargo volumes or guaranteed connections between the listed facilities. [s3]\n\nOECD research on Caspian transport identifies vessel availability, adverse weather and falling water levels as constraints on maritime movements. Its detailed operating examples concern other Caspian ports, especially in Kazakhstan, and should not be transferred to Iranian facilities as if they were local measurements. The regional lesson is nevertheless relevant: a quay and storage area are only part of a functioning route. Vessel suitability and reliable access to the water also matter. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "water-and-wetlands",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The coast is part of the infrastructure",
        "content": "The United Nations Environment Programme describes declining Caspian water levels as a concern for infrastructure, economies and ecosystems. Its working-paper abstract emphasizes that evaporation, rainfall, river discharge, water use and diversions interact, with climate change influencing these variables. This supports treating coastal change as a long-term planning issue rather than attributing it to a single cause. It does not supply a measured depth for any individual Iranian harbor. [s4]\n\nAnzali also illustrates the close relationship between a port city and a sensitive coastal environment. The Ramsar Information Sheet locates Anzali Wetland beside Bandar Anzali in Gilan Province and describes its lagoon, marshes, rivers and importance for migratory birds and fish. It records sedimentation, pollution and Caspian water-level fluctuations among the wetland’s pressures. These findings concern the wetland; they do not by themselves establish that port operations caused each pressure. [s5]\n\nUnderstanding northern ports therefore requires two kinds of evidence together: transport records showing what each facility handles, and environmental records showing the conditions in which it operates. The inspected material establishes the principal names and the importance of that environmental setting. A fuller comparison still needs port-specific cargo profiles, access depths, inland connections and consistent annual statistics.",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
