    "description": "Container shipping moves cargo in standardized intermodal containers and depends on both terminal operations and shipping services. In Iran, understanding the sector requires distinguishing container throughput from vessel capacity, port performance and the availability of trade connections.",
    "tags": ["Container shipping", "TEU", "Port performance", "Maritime logistics", "Trade statistics"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:11:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:11:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Container Shipping in Iran: TEUs, Terminals and Performance | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Learn how container shipping is measured, why throughput differs from efficiency, and what Iran’s terminal agreements can establish."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "containers-and-units",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a container statistic measures",
        "content": "Container shipping uses standardized units that can be handled by ships and terminals. Its familiar statistical abbreviation, TEU, means twenty-foot equivalent unit. The World Bank’s port performance glossary counts a standard twenty-foot container as one TEU and a forty-foot container as two. Consequently, a figure expressed in TEUs is not simply a count of physical boxes. [s1]\n\nThe same glossary distinguishes TEUs from container moves. Loading or discharging a container counts as a move regardless of its size. This matters when comparing crane activity with container volumes: two terminals can record the same number of moves while handling different TEU totals. A ship’s nominal container capacity is another separate quantity. It describes carrying capacity, rather than how many containers a port actually handled during a year. [s1]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "throughput-and-performance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Volume does not establish efficiency",
        "content": "The World Bank publishes an Iran indicator for container port traffic, identifying UN Trade and Development, or UNCTAD, as its data provider. Its national geographic scope is essential: the indicator should not be presented as the traffic of an individual terminal or shipping company. An Iran-wide total cannot establish which port gained cargo or whether a particular service was available. [s2]\n\nOperational performance requires a different lens. The World Bank’s Container Port Performance Index 2023 assesses performance using vessel time in port. Its glossary separates waiting, time alongside the berth and crane activity. These distinctions help explain why a busy port is not necessarily a fast port, and why a single annual throughput figure cannot reveal the source of delays. The index’s focus on vessels also means it should not be treated as a complete measure of a shipment’s journey beyond the terminal. [s1]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-terminal-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading Iranian terminal announcements",
        "content": "Chabahar provides a concrete example of the difference between an operating framework and a measured result. India’s shipping ministry described its May 2024 agreement with Iran as covering the equipping and operation of a general cargo and container terminal at Shahid Beheshti. The contract identifies an activity and participating institutions. It does not supply a national container traffic series or establish the availability of every onward service. [s3]\n\nA separate Indian parliamentary answer in April 2026 reported fulfillment of the equipment funding commitment under that agreement. That is a financial and contractual statement, rather than an observation of container throughput. Keeping these categories separate prevents investment announcements from being presented as completed cargo movements. [s4]\n\nFor readers assessing Iran’s container sector, the useful questions are therefore specific: which terminal is being measured, whether the unit is TEUs or moves, which reporting period applies, and whether the figure describes actual activity or available capacity. Annual comparisons also need consistent geographic coverage. The evidence inspected here supports those distinctions, but a reconciled historical series and a verified account of services at the research cutoff remain necessary before presenting a quantitative assessment of the sector. [s1][s2][s3][s4]"
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
